@page "/learn/circuit-auto-pause"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Pause hidden tabs · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Pause hidden tabs</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Staff leave tabs open all day. Why does each hidden one still use memory on your server?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> the AutoPause package. It pauses a hidden tab for you, but only when it's safe.
        .NET 10 could already pause a tab with <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, but you had to write the script
        that calls it yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        With Interactive Server, each open tab has a <i>circuit</i> on the server: its components, their state and a
        live connection. A hidden tab keeps its circuit until it's closed. Pausing frees it. The tab's
        <code>[PersistentState]</code> values are saved in the browser, and restored on a new circuit when the tab
        comes back.
    </p>
    <p>
        Below is a stock take. Posting its count to the warehouse system takes 20 seconds. Fig. 1 pauses the .NET 10
        way: its own script pauses the tab once it has been hidden for 10 seconds.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The stock take, paused by your own script" Url="demo/auto-pause/stock-take/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: your own pause script" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>+</b> on a few products, press <b>Post count</b>, then switch to another tab for about 30
            seconds. Back here, the counts are all there, but the post says <b>Not posted</b>, even though the
            warehouse has the count. Press <b>Post count</b> again and, 20 seconds later, the warehouse has it twice.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The pause lands mid-post</h2>
    <p>The script only watches the clock. Here's what happened while the tab was hidden:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The 30 seconds the tab was hidden</span>
        <pre class="snip"> 0 s  Post count pressed, tab hidden     <span class="dim">PostAsync starts: 20 s to go</span>
10 s  <span class="err">script calls Blazor.pauseCircuit()</span>  <span class="dim">state saved: Posted = null</span>
      circuit gone
20 s  warehouse takes the count           <span class="err">Posted is set on a circuit that's gone</span>
30 s  tab shown, resumeCircuit()          <span class="err">new circuit: "Not posted"</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>Posted</code> is only set when the click handler returns. But the pause saved the state ten seconds
        earlier. The post did finish on the server, but the new circuit never heard about it. So the user sees
        "Not posted" and posts again.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Let AutoPause pick the moment</h2>
    <p>
        Add the <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.AutoPause</code> package, turn it on for the page, and
        delete your own script.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="autoPause" /&gt;</span>

@@code {
    static readonly BrowserOptions autoPause = new BrowserOptions()
        <span class="hl">.AddAutoPause(o =&gt; o.HiddenDelay = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));</span>
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        That's all for one page. If a click is still being handled when the time is up, AutoPause waits for it to
        finish. Then it pauses, with the result saved.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same stock take, with AutoPause" Url="demo/auto-pause/stock-take"
                Label=".NET 11: AutoPause">
        <Caption>
            Do the same again: press <b>+</b>, press <b>Post count</b>, and switch away for about 30 seconds. Back
            here, it says <b>Posted at</b> a time, and the warehouse has the count once. AutoPause didn't pause at 10
            seconds. It waited for the post to finish at 20, so the panel shows the tab was paused for about 10
            seconds, not 20.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the script behind Fig. 1, the page behind Fig. 2, and the post they share.</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="wwwroot/js/auto-pause-net10.js" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's .NET 10 pause script. Highlighted: 10 seconds after the tab hides, it calls
            <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, no matter what's going on.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/StockTake.razor" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page. Highlighted: the new code. It turns on AutoPause with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>,
            which is also new in .NET 11. The count sheet is the same one Fig. 1 uses.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/StockPost.razor" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Highlighted, but not new: a plain click handler that takes 20 seconds. <code>Posted</code> is a
            <code>[PersistentState]</code> value, but it's only set when the handler returns.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Leave it, or pause it yourself</h2>
    <p>We tried both on .NET 10, with a page that keeps a count and a note in <code>[PersistentState]</code>:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to handle a hidden tab in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// no script</span></pre>
                <span class="why">a hidden tab keeps its circuit</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the server kept the circuit the whole time the tab was hidden</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", …)
<span class="hl">Blazor.pauseCircuit()</span></pre>
                <span class="why">pause it yourself once the tab has been hidden a while</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ freed the circuit; the count and note came back on a new one</div>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ paused mid-typing: the part of the note the server hadn't seen was lost</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The script works, but it pauses whatever the user is doing, like typing a note or a click that's still being handled.
        Checking for that was up to you.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A pause that waits its turn</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the AutoPause package watches the tab for you. Once the tab has been hidden long enough, it checks
        that nothing is in progress. If something is, it checks again every half second. Then it calls the same
        <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, and resumes when the tab comes back. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It waits for typing too.</b> It won't pause while the user has typed something the server hasn't received yet, so a half-typed note is safe. It also holds off while sound is playing, or while Picture-in-Picture or a Web Lock is in use.</li>
        <li><b>Only <code>[PersistentState]</code> survives.</b> A resumed tab gets a new circuit, so components start over and reload their data. The saved state waits in the browser, encrypted.</li>
        <li><b>For every page, use <code>Program.cs</code>:</b> <code>app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;().WithBrowserOptions(o =&gt; o.AddAutoPause())</code>. The default wait is 2 minutes.</li>
        <li><b>Pick one way.</b> If <code>Program.cs</code> calls <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> and a page uses <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>, that page's settings leak to every page, for everyone. So use one or the other, not both. (That's how .NET 11 RC2 behaves; it may change.) This app only uses <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
